speechtext:
With regard to
gold coins, of course they are full value, and legal 'tender in ordinary times really is based upon gold standard, and gold or Dominion notes may be asked for. But with regard .to 'the subsidiary coinage, silver and bronze, they do not represent the intrinsic value which they appear to have in gold. It is desirable they should approximate, particularly in the case of silver. The value of silver, of course, fluctuates from time to time, and the intrinsic value of our fifty, twenty-five, ten and five-cent pieces is not at any time equal to their face value, much less are our bronze one-cent pieces. The smaller coin has less intrinsic value;

but having regard to the fact that it is in general circulation, and is a convenience to the public, I think no serious question is likely to arise on that score. Of course, reducing the amount of bronze in it reduces its intrinsic value, but I do not think that is a serious objection to the change which we propose. Ours 'weighs a little more than the American bronze coin. As my hon. friend is interested in the subject when the Bill comes for second reading I shall obtain information as to the intrinsic value of the one-cent piece, if it is possible to estimate it, as I think it is. It is not intended to withdraw the existing coins. The Bill will provide for their continuance as legal coinage of Canada, and only as they become smooth or mutilated will they be redeemed. They will remain in circulation, for the reason I have mentioned, until they gradually disappear.

As this resolution is a very important one, I think it is proper for me to point out that, while we may agree to this resolution going through, before the Bill is introduced, there should be furnished to hon. members of this House certain information in connection with the very important transaction with which this resolution undertakes to deal. The railroad referred to in the resolution is an enterprise in which the Canadian people have taken a great interest, ever since the first steps for the building of the Grand Trunk Pacific took place in 1903 during the various years down to the present time. Now that this railway is getting into these financial and other entanglements, it is proper that the Canadian people, whose money has, to a very large extent, entered into this enterprise, should have from the Government the fullest possible information as to its exact condition. Therefore, as this is a new Parliament, I think, before the Bill is introduced, every member of this House should be furnished with a copy of the original agreement, which is not a very long one, between the Grand Trunk Pacific Railway Company and the Government; that is the agreement under which the company started in 1903, and the Government of the day undertook to furnish certain moneys and to comply with certain conditions in respect thereto. This agreement was, if I remember correctly, further amended in 1904, and a statute was passed confirming that agreement. I presume the agreement as finally confirmed and ratified in 1904 is the agreement that hon. members should have an opportunity of perusing. We should also be put in possession of the correspondence which passed between the former Government of the late Sir Wilfrid Laurier and that company, or aft least any such correspondence as has a bearing upon the reso-

lution now before the House, and also all the correspondence which passed between the Administration of the Right Hon. Sir Robert Borden and that company, in particular in reference to the section of the road that is now concerned, and also the section of the road between Winnipeg and Moncton. It is alleged that the Government of the day have not complied with the agreement entered into in 1904, and that they have made it impossible for the Grand Trunk Pacific Railway Company to carry on the enterprise. For those reasons, I think that before we deal with the question, the particulars I have enumerated should be put at the disposal of hon. members. We should also have a statement of all the financial aid, by endorsation, or guarantees of bonds, or otherwise, given by this Government or the former Government to this company, also a statement of the cash subsidies or aids granted by the different governments, from 1903 to date, to this company. The Government should give the House as clear a statement as possible as to the financial standing of the company on the 10th day of March, 1919, the date on which the Government, as I understand it, took charge.
I do not at all agree with the steps taken by the Government in regard to taking charge of this company. The War Measures Act was never intended to be used for purposes of this kind. That Act was intended only to be a ready instrument in the hands of the Government to carry out any purpose for which there was no legislative provision on the Statute Books of this country. That was a proper and necessary thing to have. But now that the war is over and there is no urgency in connection with the measure; when we have a statute of our own solemnly passed by the Parliament of this country and acted on practically since Confederation in connection with cases of this kind; when the courts of this country have a well established practice and procedure in connection with putting bodies of this kind into liquidation, it is an innovation of a most undesirable character to start a proceeding of this kind, which is bound to create entanglements and difficulties which are far-reaching and which the Government may, later on, find it very hard to remedy.
The only section in the War Measures Act that could possibly be used for an action of this kind is section 6. This section of the War Measures Act, which is Chapter II of the Statutes of Canada, 1914, reads:
The Governor in Council shall have power to do and authorize such acts and things, and to make from time to time such orders and regula-

tions as he may by reason of the existence of real or apprehended war, invasion or insurrection deem it necessary or advisable for the security, defence, peace, order and welfare of Canada; and for greater certainty, but not so as to restrict the generality of the foregoing terms, it is hereby declared that the powers of the Governor in Council shall extend to all* matters coming within the classes of subjects hereinafter enumerated, that is to say:-
(a)	Censorship and the control and suppression of publications, writings, maps, plans, photographs, communications and means of communications ;
(b)	Arrest, detention, exclusion and deportation ;
(c)	Control of the harbours, ports and territorial waters of Canada, and the movement of vessels;
(d)	Transportation by land, air or water, and the control of the transport of persons and things;
(e)	Trading, exportation, importation, Production and manufacture;
(f)	Appropriation, control, forfeiture and disposition of property and of the use thereof.
2. All orders and1 regulations made under this section shall have the force of law, and shall he enforced in such manner and by such courts, '[DOT]Hirers and authorities as the Governor in Council may prescribe, and may be varied, extended or revoked by any subsequent order oa-regulation; but if any order or regulation is varied, extended or revoked, neither the previous operation thereof nor anything duly done thereunder, shall be affected thereby, nor shall any right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued, acoruing or incurred thereunder be affected by such variation, extension or revocation.	-
All these powers could be exercised by the Government in view of the immediate danger of war. Fortunately, Sir, the danger of war is over, but the machinery was at hand whereby the Government, upon a motion before the judge, could have had a receiver appointed by the Exchequer Court instanter. That was the proper way to deal with a matter of this kind, for I submit, with all due respect to the body of men who are here to govern the affairs of this country, that this Parliament should not usurp the functions of a judge or court. I repeat, an order could have been made by a judge of the Exchequer Court and a receiver appointed in a twinkling, and when that course is followed, both the party moving for the order and the party going into liquidation has an opportunity to be heard. But in this case the pompany has not had an opportunity of being heard. The company was thrown into a receivership whether it liked it or not. For these reasons it is a very grave question for the members of this House to consider whether they will sanction this method of procedure, which is drifting away from the well-established rules that have been observed in this country at least for the last fifty years. I trust that before the Bill is introduced and we

are called upon to deckle whether the Government has taken the proper course in connection with this company, full information will be given to the House and ample opportunity provided for digesting its various phrases.
